    <!--
      The native half of the audit story (arch doc 8): who approved, when, and what
      they said. The decision log answers WHY a record routed here; this answers what
      happened next, and it is also the only surface from which an approver can act on
      a work item from the record itself.
    -->
    <relatedLists>
        <relatedList>RelatedProcessHistoryList</relatedList>
    </relatedLists>
    <!--
      EMPTY ON PURPOSE - this is arch doc 6.3's removal of the standard Submit for
      Approval button, not an oversight. With no quickActionList at all, Lightning
      falls back to a default action set that reinstates Submit for Approval the
      moment an approval process exists for the object, which is exactly when the
      guard starts to matter. Declaring the list is what suppresses that. Edit,
      Delete and Clone are unaffected: they render as standard buttons and never
      appear in a quickActionList (a stock Account layout has none of them either).

      The framework's own AMF_Submit_For_Approval action is NOT listed here because
      it cannot be: the Metadata API refuses a LightningWebComponent quick action in
      a quickActionList ("You can't add QuickActionType LightningWebComponent to a
      QuickActionList", confirmed at API 62.0 and 64.0). It is added through Setup,
      and a layout retrieved after that step will not redeploy - so do not overwrite
      this file from the org. See docs/decisions.md and technical log M3.4b.
    -->
    <quickActionList/>
    <showEmailCheckbox>false</showEmailCheckbox>
